= Module 4 — Data Transmission: Modes, Types, Media, Peripheral Control and Protocols
**Weeks 8–10 · Lessons 27–35 (+ Evaluation and Remediation No 2)**
**Category of action:** Differentiating modes of data transmission; identifying different transmission types; classification of transmission media; description of peripheral device control mechanisms; using common protocols in data transmission; executing different correctness-checking methods.
**Context:** From how devices share a medium and in which direction data flows (27), through bandwidth classes (28), serial/parallel, synchronous/asynchronous and analog/digital (29), guided and unguided media (30), peripheral control (31), protocols and TCP/IP (32), the OSI model (33), an integration activity (34), Evaluation No 2, to error detection and parity (35).

# Lesson 27: Modes of Data Transmission

> OBJ:
  - Explain logical topology methods (CSMA/CD, CSMA/CA).
  - Describe different transmission modes (simplex, half-duplex, full duplex).
  - Illustrate the direction of signal flow for a given data transmission scenario.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 6 min | Everyone talk at once for 10 seconds; then use a "raise hand" rule; then two people on walkie-talkies saying "over". | Experience the ideas. |
| Core 1 | 34 min | Logical topology vs physical; media-access methods: **CSMA/CD** (Ethernet on shared media/hubs: listen, transmit, detect collision, jam, random back-off; why switches made it obsolete), **CSMA/CA** (Wi-Fi: listen, wait DIFS + random back-off, optional RTS/CTS, ACK; hidden-node problem; why collisions cannot be detected in radio); token passing and polling as alternatives; logical bus vs logical ring. Simulation game. | Play protocol games. |
| Core 2 | 30 min | Transmission modes: **simplex** (one direction only), **half-duplex** (both directions, one at a time), **full-duplex** (both simultaneously); examples; relation to media/devices (hub half-duplex, switch full-duplex; Wi-Fi half-duplex; fibre pairs full-duplex); bandwidth implications. | Classify devices. |
| Core 3 | 22 min | Illustrating signal-flow direction: diagrams with arrows for scenarios (TV broadcast, keyboard→PC, walkie-talkie, phone call, video call, sensor to controller, printer status, Wi-Fi client–AP, switch port). | Draw scenarios. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 27.1 Logical topology and media-access methods

> DEF:
  The **logical topology** describes **how data actually flows and how devices gain access to the shared medium**, regardless of the physical wiring. When several devices share one medium (a coaxial bus, a hub, a Wi-Fi channel), a **media access control (MAC) method** decides who may transmit and how collisions are handled.

| Method | Full name | Where used | How it works |
| **CSMA/CD** | Carrier Sense Multiple Access with **Collision Detection** | Classic Ethernet on coaxial bus or hubs (half-duplex, logical bus); IEEE 802.3 | 1. **Carrier sense**: listen – if the medium is busy, wait. 2. **Multiple access**: if idle, transmit. 3. **Collision detection**: while sending, monitor the wire; if another signal overlaps (voltage anomaly) a **collision** occurred. 4. Send a **jam signal** so all stations notice. 5. Each colliding station waits a **random back-off** time (binary exponential back-off – doubles range after each collision) and retries. Effective when the medium is short and load is moderate; performance collapses under heavy load. **Switched full-duplex Ethernet has no collisions, so CSMA/CD is now dormant** |
| **CSMA/CA** | Carrier Sense Multiple Access with **Collision Avoidance** | **Wi-Fi** (IEEE 802.11), some sensor networks | Radios cannot transmit and listen simultaneously on one channel, so collisions cannot be detected – they must be **avoided**: 1. Listen; if busy, wait until idle. 2. Wait an inter-frame space (**DIFS**) plus a **random back-off** counted only while idle. 3. Transmit the frame. 4. Receiver replies with an **ACK**; no ACK → assume collision/loss and retry with longer back-off. Optional **RTS/CTS** handshake (Request/Clear to Send) reserves the channel and solves the **hidden-node problem** (two clients that cannot hear each other but both reach the AP). Overheads make Wi-Fi **half-duplex** and less efficient than wired |
| Token passing | – | Token Ring, FDDI, some industrial networks (logical ring) | A special frame (token) circulates; only the holder transmits – no collisions, deterministic delay |
| Polling | – | Mainframe terminals, Bluetooth master–slave, some IoT | A controller asks each device in turn whether it has data |

Logical vs physical examples: a hub-based **physical star** is a **logical bus** (everyone hears everything; CSMA/CD); Token Ring wired as a physical star through a MAU is a **logical ring**; a switched star is point-to-point per port (no shared medium); Wi-Fi around an AP is a physical star but a logical shared bus (CSMA/CA).

### 27.2 Transmission modes

> DEF:
  The **transmission mode** (direction mode) defines the **direction(s) in which data can flow between two devices**: **simplex** – one direction only; **half-duplex** – both directions but only one at a time; **full-duplex** – both directions simultaneously.

| Mode | Direction of flow | Characteristics | Examples |
| **Simplex** | A → B only; B never sends back | Full channel capacity in one direction; no feedback/acknowledgement possible on the same link | Radio and TV broadcasting; keyboard → computer; mouse → computer; computer → monitor/projector; sensor → data logger; pager; public-address system; one-way fibre in some CCTV systems |
| **Half-duplex** | A ↔ B, one at a time (turn-taking) | Shared channel; devices must switch between send and receive; needs a protocol for turns (CSMA, "over") | Walkie-talkies/two-way radio; **Wi-Fi** on one channel; classic Ethernet with hubs; Bluetooth (time-division); USB 2.0; RS-485 buses; citizen band radio; a single-lane bridge with signals |
| **Full-duplex** | A ↔ B simultaneously | Two channels (separate wires/fibres, frequency or time separation); highest throughput; no turn-taking | Telephone calls (landline/mobile); **switched Ethernet** (separate transmit/receive pairs, 1 Gbps each way); fibre links (two fibres); video calls; USB 3.x; full-duplex radio with different frequencies (FDD 4G) |

Device notes: a **hub** forces half-duplex; a **switch** port negotiates full-duplex (mismatch causes late collisions/slow links); **Wi-Fi 6/7** still half-duplex per channel though MU-MIMO/OFDMA serve several clients at once; mobile networks achieve full-duplex via separate uplink/downlink frequencies (FDD) or time slots (TDD).

### 27.3 Illustrating signal flow for scenarios

| Scenario | Mode | Diagram description (arrows) |
| CRTV television broadcast to homes | Simplex | Transmitter → many receivers; single arrowhead |
| Keyboard typing into a PC | Simplex | Keyboard → PC (status LEDs are a separate simplex control channel) |
| Security guards using walkie-talkies | Half-duplex | A → B while push-to-talk; then B → A; double arrow with "one at a time" label |
| Phone call between Yaoundé and Douala | Full-duplex | A ⇄ B simultaneous double arrows |
| Laptop on Wi-Fi downloading while uploading | Half-duplex at the radio level | Laptop ⇄ AP with alternating slots; CSMA/CA back-off boxes |
| PC to switch to server (Gigabit Ethernet) | Full-duplex | PC ⇄ switch ⇄ server on separate pairs |
| Temperature sensor → irrigation controller | Simplex | Sensor → controller |
| Controller → valve actuator | Simplex | Controller → valve |
| Video conference | Full-duplex | Both cameras/mics streaming simultaneously |
| Printer receiving a job and reporting "paper out" | Full- or half-duplex depending on interface (USB 2.0 half; Ethernet full) | Two arrows with interface note |
| 20 PCs on a hub | Half-duplex, logical bus, CSMA/CD | Star drawn; all frames broadcast; collision burst illustration |

> EXAMPLE:
  **A school assembly and its network.** The principal on the PA system is **simplex** (nobody can reply through the speakers). Prefects coordinating on walkie-talkies are **half-duplex** ("over"). The ICT teacher's phone call to a supplier is **full-duplex**. In the lab, 40 PCs on a Gigabit switch are full-duplex point-to-point links; the staffroom laptops on Wi-Fi share one channel with **CSMA/CA** – which is why 30 simultaneous video streams stutter while 30 wired PCs do not.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 27.1 – CSMA/CD flowchart** (listen → idle? → transmit → collision? → jam → random back-off → retry) and **CSMA/CA flowchart** (listen → DIFS + back-off → transmit → ACK received? → retry); a hidden-node sketch (two laptops far apart, AP in the middle) with RTS/CTS arrows. **Figure 27.2 – Three modes**: one-lane one-way road (simplex), one-lane two-way road with signals (half-duplex), two-lane road (full-duplex), each paired with a device example. **Figure 27.3 – Scenario sheet** of eight diagrams with arrowheads to complete.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "Wi-Fi is full-duplex because I can download and upload at the same time." At the radio level frames alternate very fast – it is half-duplex; the apparent simultaneity is time sharing. "Collisions happen on switched networks." Full-duplex switched ports eliminate them; CSMA/CD only mattered on shared media.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Protocol games and diagrams (45 min).** (1) CSMA/CD game: eight learners around a "bus" (rope); message cards; a collision occurs when two speak in the same beat – both roll dice for back-off; count successful frames per minute at low vs high load. (2) CSMA/CA game: learners cannot hear those beyond two seats (hidden nodes); add RTS/CTS via a referee "AP" and compare collisions. (3) Draw signal-flow diagrams for ten scenario cards and label the mode; justify two ambiguous ones (USB, Bluetooth). (4) Lab check: read the duplex setting of a PC NIC (`Get-NetAdapter | fl` / `ethtool eth0`) and the Wi-Fi channel; explain each.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Explain CSMA/CD step by step and why it is no longer needed on modern switched Ethernet.
  2. Explain CSMA/CA and why Wi-Fi uses avoidance rather than detection; what problem does RTS/CTS solve?
  3. Distinguish logical from physical topology with the hub-star example.
  4. Describe simplex, half-duplex and full-duplex with two examples each.
  5. Illustrate with arrow diagrams the signal flow for: a TV broadcast; walkie-talkies; a Gigabit Ethernet link; a Wi-Fi client; a keyboard.
  6. Why is a duplex mismatch between a PC and a switch a problem?
  **Homework:** Find eight devices/systems at home or school, sketch their signal flow with arrows and classify the mode; note which use a shared medium and the access method.

