# Lesson 28: Broadband and Narrowband Transmission

> OBJ:
  - Define broadband and narrowband transmission.
  - Differentiate between broadband and narrowband transmissions.
  - Identify the appropriate transmission type used in a given communication situation.

## Lesson outline (1 period, 50 min)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 4 min | Compare a garden hose and a river; an SMS and a Netflix stream. | Discuss. |
| Core 1 | 14 min | Definitions: bandwidth (Hz and bit/s), **narrowband** (small bandwidth, single channel/low data rate – baseband signalling often), **broadband** (wide bandwidth, high data rate, multiple channels multiplexed – FDM/OFDM); technical vs regulatory definitions (ITU/regulators: broadband ≥ 256 kbps historically, now commonly ≥ 25/3 Mbps or more); baseband vs broadband signalling (Ethernet is baseband; cable/DSL/4G are broadband). | Notes. |
| Core 2 | 16 min | Differentiation table: bandwidth, data rate, channels, technologies, cost, applications, latency, coverage; examples: narrowband – telephone voice channel (300–3,400 Hz), dial-up 56 kbps, GSM SMS/2G GPRS, LoRaWAN/NB-IoT sensors, VHF radio, telemetry, satellite phone; broadband – ADSL/VDSL, fibre FTTH, cable, 4G/5G, Wi-Fi, satellite broadband (Starlink), leased lines. | Complete table. |
| Core 3 | 12 min | Identifying the appropriate type for situations: video conferencing school, IoT soil sensors, emergency voice radio, rural clinic e-mail, e-learning platform, smart meter readings, CCTV streaming, MoMo USSD transaction. | Scenario matching. |
| Conclusion | 4 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 28.1 Definitions

> DEF:
  **Bandwidth** is the capacity of a channel – the range of frequencies it can carry (Hz) or, in practice, the maximum data rate (bit/s). **Narrowband transmission** uses a **small bandwidth/low data rate**, typically **one channel** at a time, suited to voice, text and small sensor data. **Broadband transmission** uses a **wide bandwidth** carrying **large data rates and often many simultaneous channels** (voice, video, internet) by **multiplexing** (frequency-division, OFDM, wavelength-division) – suited to multimedia and internet services.

Related distinction: **baseband** signalling sends one digital signal occupying the whole cable (Ethernet, USB); **broadband** signalling modulates data onto carrier frequencies so many signals share the medium (cable TV/internet, DSL, cellular, Wi-Fi). Regulatory thresholds for "broadband internet" have risen from ITU's historic 256 kbps to typical national definitions of 25–100 Mbps download.

### 28.2 Differentiating broadband and narrowband

| Criterion | Narrowband | Broadband |
| **Bandwidth** | Small (e.g. 3 kHz voice; up to a few hundred kHz) | Wide (MHz to GHz) |
| **Data rate** | Low – bit/s to a few hundred kbit/s (dial-up 56 kbps; GPRS 40–80 kbps; NB-IoT ~ 250 kbps; LoRa 0.3–50 kbps) | High – Mbit/s to Gbit/s (ADSL 8–24 Mbps; 4G 10–100 Mbps; fibre 100 Mbps–10 Gbps; 5G up to Gbps) |
| **Channels** | Usually a single channel; one service at a time (voice OR data) | Many channels multiplexed simultaneously (voice + video + data) |
| **Signalling** | Often baseband or simple modulation | Modulated onto carriers (FDM/OFDM/QAM), WDM on fibre |
| **Latency/robustness** | Can be robust over long distances and noise; long range at low power (IoT radios) | Higher rates need better signal quality; range/wall penetration trade-offs |
| **Cost & power** | Low cost, low power (batteries last years) | Higher equipment/subscription cost, more power |
| **Typical technologies** | PSTN voice line, dial-up modem, 2G GSM/GPRS, SMS/USSD, VHF/UHF two-way radio, pagers, telemetry/SCADA links, LoRaWAN, Sigfox, NB-IoT, satellite phones, RS-232 links | ADSL/VDSL, FTTH/GPON fibre, cable (DOCSIS), 3G/4G/5G mobile broadband, Wi-Fi, WiMAX, microwave links, satellite broadband (VSAT, Starlink), leased lines/MPLS |
| **Applications** | Voice calls, SMS/USSD (mobile money), sensor readings, alarms, GPS trackers, emergency radio, simple e-mail | Web browsing, video streaming/conferencing, cloud services, e-learning, gaming, CCTV, large file transfer, VoIP at scale |
| **Coverage in Cameroon** | Near-universal (GSM, radio) | Growing in cities (fibre, 4G); patchy in rural areas |

### 28.3 Identifying the appropriate transmission type

| Communication situation | Appropriate type | Reasoning |
| School video conferencing and e-learning platform | **Broadband** (fibre/4G ≥ 20 Mbps) | High data rate, multiple streams |
| Soil-moisture sensors on a cocoa farm reporting hourly | **Narrowband** (LoRaWAN/NB-IoT/SMS) | Tiny data, long battery life, long range, low cost |
| Emergency voice communication for forest rangers | **Narrowband** (VHF/UHF two-way radio, satellite phone) | Robust, wide coverage, no infrastructure |
| Rural health centre sending weekly reports by e-mail | Narrowband acceptable (2G/GPRS/SMS-based DHIS2) ; broadband if telemedicine | Small text data; upgrade for imaging |
| Mobile-money transaction via USSD/SMS | **Narrowband** | Works on any phone/2G; a few bytes |
| Streaming CCTV from 16 cameras to a security room | **Broadband** (Gigabit LAN/fibre) | Continuous video |
| Smart electricity-meter readings | **Narrowband** (NB-IoT/GPRS/PLC) | Periodic small packets |
| City hospital PACS imaging and telemedicine | **Broadband** (fibre, leased line) | Large images, real-time video |
| Weather station telemetry from a remote mountain | **Narrowband** (radio/satellite telemetry) | Low volume, remote |
| Household of five streaming, gaming and working from home | **Broadband** (FTTH/4G/5G) | Multiple simultaneous high-rate services |

> EXAMPLE:
  **One cooperative, two networks.** The coffee cooperative in Dschang installs **LoRaWAN narrowband** sensors on drying beds (temperature/humidity every 15 minutes, batteries lasting 3 years, gateway 8 km away) and a **fibre broadband** line at the office for the e-commerce site, video calls with buyers in Europe and cloud accounting. Using 4G broadband modems for sensors would have cost more, drained batteries and failed where coverage is weak; using narrowband for video calls is impossible.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 28.1 – Pipe analogy**: narrow pipe with one trickle (voice/SMS) vs wide pipe subdivided into many channels (video, voice, data) – FDM shown as coloured bands on a frequency axis. **Figure 28.2 – Data-rate scale (log)**: dial-up, GPRS, NB-IoT, 3G, ADSL, 4G, Wi-Fi, fibre, 5G positioned with icons. **Figure 28.3 – Cameroon coverage sketch**: GSM everywhere, 4G/fibre in cities, LoRa/satellite for rural sensors.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "Narrowband is obsolete." IoT, mobile money (USSD) and emergency radio rely on it because it is cheap, robust and long-range. "Broadband means fibre only." Any wide-band, multiplexed, high-rate medium qualifies – 4G/5G, cable, satellite, Wi-Fi.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Match the channel (30 min).** (1) Measure: run a speed test on the lab connection and on a 2G/3G phone (if available); place results on the log scale. (2) Card sort: 16 technology cards into narrowband/broadband with data rate estimates. (3) Scenario clinic: for ten situations choose the type and specific technology, justify with data volume, coverage, power and cost; present two contrasting cases.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Define bandwidth, narrowband transmission and broadband transmission.
  2. Differentiate broadband from narrowband on bandwidth, data rate, channels, cost and applications.
  3. Distinguish baseband from broadband signalling with an example of each.
  4. Give four narrowband and four broadband technologies with typical data rates.
  5. Identify the appropriate transmission type for: farm sensors; telemedicine; USSD mobile money; a smart city CCTV network; ranger radios – justify.
  6. Why does mobile money still use narrowband channels?
  **Homework:** List the communication services you used this week, classify each as narrowband or broadband, estimate the data volume and identify the technology.

