# Lesson 30: Transmission Media (Guided and Unguided)

> OBJ:
  - Describe guided (wired) transmission media (e.g., copper wire, coaxial cable, optical fibre…).
  - Describe unguided (wireless) transmission media (e.g., Bluetooth, microwave, Wi-Fi…).
  - Choose an appropriate transmission medium for a given context.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min – samples of cables)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 5 min | Pass round UTP, coaxial, fibre patch cord; show a Wi-Fi AP, Bluetooth speaker, satellite dish photo. | Handle; guess speeds. |
| Core 1 | 35 min | **Guided media**: twisted pair (UTP/STP; categories 5e/6/6a/7/8; RJ-45; 100 m; 1–40 Gbps; cost; noise), coaxial (RG-6/RG-59; cable TV/internet, CCTV, antennas; shielding), **optical fibre** (single-mode vs multi-mode; total internal reflection; km distances; Tbps; immune to EMI; fragile/cost; connectors SC/LC; submarine cables landing in Cameroon), also power-line and legacy telephone line (DSL). Construction, how signals travel, advantages/disadvantages. | Media fact cards. |
| Core 2 | 30 min | **Unguided media** (electromagnetic waves through air/space): radio waves (**Wi-Fi** 2.4/5/6 GHz standards 802.11n/ac/ax/be, **Bluetooth** 2.4 GHz classes, cellular 2G–5G, LoRa, AM/FM), **microwave** (terrestrial point-to-point links, line of sight, 1–40 GHz; satellite GEO/MEO/LEO – VSAT, Starlink), infrared (remote controls, IrDA), NFC/RFID; frequency vs range/penetration; licensing (ART). | Spectrum diagram. |
| Core 3 | 22 min | Choosing a medium: criteria (distance, bandwidth, cost, interference/EMI, security, mobility, installation constraints, environment, scalability); decision table; scenarios (lab desks, two buildings 300 m apart, village 40 km from fibre, ISP backbone, mobile clinic, IoT sensors, hospital MRI room). | Scenario decisions. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 30.1 Guided (wired) transmission media

> DEF:
  **Guided media** confine signals to a **physical path** (copper conductor or glass/plastic fibre). **Unguided media** transmit **electromagnetic waves through air or space** without a physical conductor (radio, microwave, infrared, light).

| Medium | Construction & signal | Standards / speeds | Max distance | Advantages | Disadvantages | Uses |
| **Twisted-pair copper (UTP/STP)** | 4 pairs of insulated copper wires twisted to cancel interference; **UTP** unshielded, **STP/FTP** with foil/braid; electrical signals; RJ-45 connectors | Cat 5e (1 Gbps), **Cat 6** (1 Gbps/10 Gbps to 55 m), Cat 6a (10 Gbps), Cat 7/8 (25–40 Gbps short); telephone Cat 3 | **100 m** per segment (90 m + patch cords) | Cheap, easy to install/terminate, flexible, ubiquitous, supports PoE | Limited distance, susceptible to EMI/crosstalk (UTP), can be tapped, lower bandwidth than fibre | LAN desks, patch panels, phones, PoE cameras/APs, DSL local loop |
| **Coaxial cable** | Central copper conductor, dielectric insulator, braided/foil **shield**, outer jacket; signal between core and shield | RG-59 (CCTV), RG-6 (TV/cable internet – DOCSIS 3.1 up to 10 Gbps shared), 10BASE2/5 legacy Ethernet | 100s of metres (185–500 m Ethernet legacy; km for CATV with amplifiers) | Good shielding against EMI, higher bandwidth than UTP, durable | Bulky, harder to install, expensive per metre vs UTP, single core (shared bus historically) | Cable TV/internet, satellite dish feeds, CCTV, antenna links, some broadband distribution |
| **Optical fibre** | Glass (or plastic) core + cladding + buffer/jacket; **light pulses** (laser/LED) guided by **total internal reflection**; **single-mode** (9 µm core, laser, long distance) vs **multi-mode** (50/62.5 µm, LED/VCSEL, shorter); connectors SC, LC, ST | 1–100 Gbps per wavelength; **WDM** → Tbps; 1000BASE-LX/SX, 10GBASE-LR/SR | Multi-mode 300–550 m (10 G); **single-mode 10–80 km** without repeater; submarine thousands of km with amplifiers | **Huge bandwidth**, **immune to EMI** and lightning-induced currents, very low attenuation, secure (hard to tap), light and thin | Higher cost of equipment/splicing, fragile (bend radius), skilled installation, cannot carry power | Backbones between buildings/floors, MAN/WAN, ISP and submarine cables (SAT-3/WACS at Douala, ACE at Kribi, SAIL Kribi–Fortaleza), FTTH (Camtel Blue), data centres, hospital imaging |
| Power-line (PLC/HomePlug) | Data over electrical wiring | 200 Mbps–2 Gbps nominal | Within a building | No new cabling | Noise, variable | Homes |
| Telephone line (DSL) | Existing copper pair | ADSL 24 Mbps, VDSL 100 Mbps | 1–5 km from exchange | Reuses lines | Distance-dependent | Legacy broadband |

### 30.2 Unguided (wireless) transmission media

| Medium | Frequency / range | Standards / speeds | Characteristics | Uses |
| **Radio – Wi-Fi (WLAN)** | 2.4 GHz (longer range, more interference, 3 non-overlapping channels), 5 GHz (faster, shorter), 6 GHz (Wi-Fi 6E/7); indoor 20–50 m, outdoor 100 m+ | 802.11n (Wi-Fi 4, 600 Mbps), **802.11ac** (Wi-Fi 5, Gbps), **802.11ax** (Wi-Fi 6/6E, 9.6 Gbps), 802.11be (Wi-Fi 7) | Licence-free, shared half-duplex medium (CSMA/CA), walls attenuate, security via WPA2/WPA3 | Homes, offices, schools, hotspots |
| **Radio – Bluetooth (WPAN)** | 2.4 GHz; Class 1 ≈ 100 m, Class 2 ≈ 10 m; BLE for low power | Bluetooth 5.x: 2 Mbps (BLE), up to 3 Mbps classic | Low power, frequency hopping (Hedy Lamarr's idea), pairing security | Headsets, wearables, keyboards, file transfer, IoT beacons |
| **Radio – cellular (2G–5G)** | 700 MHz–3.5 GHz (5G mmWave 26 GHz+); cell radius 1–30 km | 2G GPRS/EDGE (kbps), 3G (Mbps), **4G LTE** (10–100+ Mbps), **5G** (100 Mbps–Gbps, low latency) | Licensed spectrum (MTN, Orange, Camtel via ART), mobility, wide coverage | Mobile broadband, MoMo, IoT (NB-IoT/LTE-M) |
| **Radio – LPWAN (LoRa, Sigfox, NB-IoT)** | Sub-GHz (868 MHz); 2–15 km | 0.3–50 kbps | Very low power, long range, tiny data | Agriculture, metering, tracking |
| **Radio – broadcast & two-way** | AM/FM/VHF/UHF | Analog/digital audio; DVB-T2 TV | Wide coverage | Radio/TV, ranger/police radios |
| **Microwave – terrestrial** | 1–40 GHz; **line of sight** between towers/dishes, 30–50 km per hop | 100 Mbps–10 Gbps per link | Fast to deploy vs fibre trenching, affected by rain (higher bands), obstacles, needs masts; licensed bands (or 5 GHz/60 GHz licence-free) | Linking buildings/towns, mobile-tower backhaul, ISP links in hilly regions (West, North-West) |
| **Microwave – satellite** | C/Ku/Ka bands 4–30 GHz; **GEO** 36,000 km (latency ~600 ms), **MEO**, **LEO** (Starlink ~550 km, latency 20–50 ms) | VSAT 1–50 Mbps; LEO 50–200+ Mbps | Coverage anywhere (rural, sea, disaster), expensive per Mbps, rain fade, dish alignment | Rural connectivity, banks/ATMs in remote areas, broadcasting, backup links, GPS (one-way) |
| **Infrared** | 300 GHz–400 THz light; line of sight, few metres | IrDA up to 4–16 Mbps (legacy) | Cannot pass walls (secure, no interference), cheap | Remote controls, some sensors, short links |
| **NFC / RFID** | 13.56 MHz (NFC, < 10 cm); RFID LF/HF/UHF up to ~10 m | kbps | Very short range, contactless | Payments, ID cards, attendance, inventory |
| **Free-space optics / Li-Fi** | Visible/IR light beams | Gbps | Line of sight, weather sensitive | Building links, research |

General rule: **lower frequencies travel further and penetrate walls better but carry less data; higher frequencies carry more data but need line of sight and are absorbed by rain, walls and foliage.**

### 30.3 Choosing an appropriate medium

| Criterion | Ask | Favours |
| **Distance** | Metres, hundreds of metres, kilometres? | UTP ≤ 100 m; fibre/microwave for longer; satellite for anywhere |
| **Bandwidth needed** | Mbps or Gbps? growth? | Fibre > UTP Cat 6 > Wi-Fi > cellular > LPWAN |
| **Cost** | Budget for materials, installation, subscriptions | UTP cheapest per port; wireless avoids trenching; satellite highest per Mbps |
| **Interference / environment** | Motors, lightning, MRI, rain, dust, hills | Fibre immune to EMI; STP in noisy plants; microwave suffers rain; Wi-Fi 2.4 GHz crowded |
| **Security** | Sensitive data? | Fibre hardest to tap; wired better than wireless; wireless needs strong encryption |
| **Mobility** | Do users move? | Wi-Fi, cellular, Bluetooth |
| **Installation constraints** | Can we dig/trunk? heritage building? rivers/roads to cross? | Wireless bridges, microwave; PLC |
| **Reliability & availability** | Uptime needs; power at repeaters | Fibre very reliable; wireless weather-dependent; dual media for redundancy |
| **Scalability** | Future upgrades | Fibre (upgrade optics not cable), Cat 6a |
| **Regulation** | Licensed spectrum? | Licence-free Wi-Fi/5 GHz vs ART-licensed microwave/cellular |

| Context | Recommended medium | Justification |
| Desks in a computer lab | **UTP Cat 6** to a switch | Short runs, cheap, Gigabit, PoE |
| Staffroom and library mobile devices | **Wi-Fi 6** APs | Mobility; wired backhaul |
| Two school blocks 300 m apart | **Single-mode fibre** (or 5 GHz point-to-point bridge if trenching impossible) | > 100 m, lightning isolation, future bandwidth |
| Village clinic 40 km from the nearest fibre, hilly | **Microwave link** if line of sight; else **LEO satellite (Starlink)** or 4G | Distance, terrain, cost |
| ISP backbone Douala–Yaoundé | **Fibre** | Tbps capacity, reliability |
| Wireless headset for a receptionist | **Bluetooth** | Personal range, low power |
| Cocoa-farm sensors over 10 km | **LoRaWAN** | Long range, low power, tiny data |
| Hospital MRI room and factory floor with heavy motors | **Fibre** (or STP for short runs) | EMI immunity |
| Mobile-money agent in a market | **Cellular 3G/4G** | Mobility, coverage |
| Home in an old rented house (no drilling allowed) | **Wi-Fi mesh** or **power-line** adapters | No cabling |
| Bank ATM in a remote town | **VSAT** with 4G backup | Coverage and redundancy |

> EXAMPLE:
  **Connecting Lycée de Mbouda's new science block (250 m across a road).** Options: UTP – impossible (> 100 m, road); coaxial – legacy, no; **single-mode fibre in a duct under the road** – 1 Gbps now, 10 Gbps later, lightning-safe, but trenching permit and splicing needed (≈ 600,000 XAF); **5 GHz wireless bridge** – installed in a day for 150,000 XAF, 300–800 Mbps, but rain/interference risk and half-duplex; **microwave licensed link** – overkill. Decision: wireless bridge now (budget, speed of deployment), fibre when the road is next dug – with the bridge kept as backup. The criteria table, not fashion, drove the choice.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 30.1 – Cable cross-sections**: UTP (4 twisted pairs, RJ-45 pinout), coaxial (core, dielectric, shield, jacket), fibre (core, cladding, buffer; light zig-zagging by total internal reflection; single vs multi-mode). **Figure 30.2 – Electromagnetic spectrum band** from radio (AM/FM, LoRa, cellular, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth) through microwave (terrestrial, satellite) to infrared/visible light, annotated with range vs data-rate trend. **Figure 30.3 – Media decision matrix** (contexts × criteria) and a campus map showing UTP in buildings, fibre between blocks, wireless bridge to the annex, Wi-Fi zones, and a satellite/4G backup.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "Fibre is fragile so unreliable." Properly installed fibre is the most reliable medium; only careless bending or digging harms it. "Wireless is slower than cable in all cases." Modern microwave/Wi-Fi 6 can exceed old Cat 5e; but shared, half-duplex and weather-affected wireless is less predictable than fibre/UTP.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Media lab and decision workshop (50 min).** (1) Identify and describe five media samples (construction, connector, speed, distance); terminate a UTP cable with T568B and test it (if tools available). (2) Wi-Fi survey: use a phone analyser app to record signal strength and channels in three locations; explain 2.4 vs 5 GHz findings. (3) Scenario decisions: for eight contexts choose and justify the medium with the criteria table; include cost estimates from a price list. (4) Map the media used from a lab PC to a website: UTP → fibre backbone → ISP fibre → submarine cable → … and note where wireless might replace each hop.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Describe UTP, coaxial cable and optical fibre (construction, speed, distance, advantages, disadvantages, uses).
  2. Distinguish single-mode from multi-mode fibre and explain total internal reflection.
  3. Describe Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, cellular, terrestrial microwave and satellite as unguided media, with frequency, range and uses.
  4. Why do higher frequencies carry more data but travel less far? Give two consequences for network design.
  5. Choose and justify a medium for: lab desks; two buildings 400 m apart; a village 50 km from fibre; sensors on a plantation; an MRI room.
  6. State five criteria for choosing a transmission medium.
  **Homework:** Produce a poster (A4) of guided vs unguided media with a labelled diagram of each medium and a "when to use" box; add one Cameroonian example per medium (e.g. WACS cable at Limbe, Camtel FTTH, MTN 4G, ranger VHF).

