# Lesson 31: Peripheral Device Control Mechanisms

> OBJ:
  - Explain the meaning and role of buffers, interrupts, interrupt priorities, polling and handshaking in controlling peripheral devices.
  - Differentiate between the various techniques used to control communication between the computer and peripheral devices.
  - Determine the technique used to control a device in a given network scenario.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 5 min | Why can you keep typing while a document prints and a download continues? Why does a 3 GHz CPU not wait for a 30-page-per-minute printer? | Hypothesise. |
| Core 1 | 40 min | The speed gap between CPU and peripherals; **buffers** (temporary memory holding data between devices of different speeds – printer buffer, keyboard buffer, network card ring buffers, video streaming buffer, spooling; double buffering), **interrupts** (signal from device to CPU requesting attention; interrupt handler/ISR; saving state; IRQ lines; hardware vs software interrupts), **interrupt priorities** (which interrupt is served first – power failure > clock > disk > network > keyboard > printer; nested interrupts, masking), **polling** (CPU repeatedly checks device status – simple but wastes CPU time; used for very fast or simple devices, USB host polling, some sensors), **handshaking** (exchange of control signals to agree readiness – RTS/CTS, ACK/NAK, XON/XOFF; hardware and software flow control; TCP three-way handshake as network handshaking), DMA as a related mechanism. | Concept map. |
| Core 2 | 30 min | Differentiating techniques: interrupt-driven vs polling (CPU efficiency, latency, complexity, suitability), buffering vs handshaking (data staging vs readiness signalling), software vs hardware flow control, programmed I/O vs DMA; comparison table; typical device–technique pairing. | Comparison table. |
| Core 3 | 17 min | Determining the technique in network scenarios: printer on the LAN with print spooler (buffer + handshaking), NIC receiving frames (interrupts + ring buffer, interrupt coalescing), Wi-Fi RTS/CTS (handshaking), TCP connection set-up (handshake) and flow control (window = buffer), USB webcam (host polling), keyboard (interrupt), UPS signalling power loss (high-priority interrupt), streaming video (buffer), sensor network gateway polling nodes. | Scenario worksheet. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 31.1 Mechanisms for controlling peripheral devices

> KEY:
  Peripherals (keyboard, printer, disk, network card, camera) are **much slower than the CPU** and operate **asynchronously**. Control mechanisms let the CPU and devices cooperate efficiently: **buffers** absorb speed differences, **interrupts** let devices call the CPU only when needed, **interrupt priorities** decide urgency, **polling** lets the CPU check devices on a schedule, and **handshaking** lets devices agree they are ready before data flows.

| Mechanism | Meaning | Role | Examples |
| **Buffer** | A **temporary storage area** (in RAM or on the device) that holds data while it is transferred between two devices or processes working at **different speeds** or times | Smooths speed mismatch; lets the CPU dump data and continue; handles bursts; enables **spooling** (jobs queued on disk for a printer); **double buffering** lets one buffer fill while the other empties (video, graphics) | **Printer buffer** (document stored so the PC is free while pages print), **keyboard buffer** (keystrokes typed faster than processed), **NIC receive/transmit ring buffers**, disk cache, **video-streaming buffer** (a few seconds ahead to survive network jitter), sound-card buffer, TCP send/receive windows |
| **Interrupt** | A **signal sent by a device (or software) to the CPU** indicating it needs attention – data ready, operation complete, error | CPU **suspends** the current program, **saves its state**, runs the **interrupt service routine (ISR/handler)** for that device, then **restores** and resumes; avoids the CPU waiting idly; supports multitasking | Keyboard key press, mouse movement, **NIC "frame received"**, disk "transfer complete", timer/clock tick, printer "out of paper", UPS "power failure", **software interrupts/system calls**, exceptions (divide by zero) |
| **Interrupt priorities** | Each interrupt source has a **priority level**; when several arrive together, or a new one arrives during a handler, the **highest priority is served first**; lower ones wait (or higher ones **nest**/pre-empt); some can be **masked** temporarily | Ensures critical events (power failure, hardware faults) are never delayed by trivial ones (printer ready) | Typical order: power failure/hardware error > system clock/timer > disk/DMA > network > keyboard/mouse > printer > low-priority devices; managed by the **interrupt controller (PIC/APIC)** and OS |
| **Polling** | The CPU (or controller) **periodically checks the status register of a device** ("Do you have data? Are you ready?") in a loop or at intervals | Simple hardware, predictable timing; but **wastes CPU cycles** when nothing is ready and adds latency between polls; best for very fast/simple devices or when interrupt rates would be overwhelming | **USB host polling** of devices (mouse polled every 1–8 ms), simple microcontroller sensor loops (Arduino `digitalRead` in `loop()`), SNMP monitoring polling switches, gateways polling sensor nodes, legacy parallel port status |
| **Handshaking** | An **exchange of control signals** between sender and receiver to **establish readiness, synchronise and confirm** transfer ("Ready to send?" – "Clear to send" – data – "Acknowledged") | Prevents data loss/overflow (**flow control**), confirms link parameters, detects errors (ACK/NAK) | Hardware: **RTS/CTS** and DTR/DSR lines on serial ports; **STROBE/BUSY/ACK** lines on the old printer port; software: **XON/XOFF** characters; network: **TCP three-way handshake** (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK), **Wi-Fi RTS/CTS**, TLS handshake, Bluetooth pairing, HDMI/USB negotiation of capabilities |
| Related: **DMA** (Direct Memory Access) | A DMA controller transfers data between a device and memory **without the CPU** moving each byte; the CPU is interrupted only when the block is complete | High-speed disk, network and graphics transfers | NIC/SSD transfers |
| Related: **Device driver & I/O controller** | Software/hardware that implements these mechanisms for a specific device | Abstraction for the OS | Printer driver, NIC driver |

### 31.2 Differentiating the techniques

| Comparison | Distinction |
| **Interrupt-driven I/O vs polling** | Interrupt: device initiates, CPU free until needed – **efficient**, low latency, more complex hardware/software (handlers, priorities). Polling: CPU initiates checks – **simple, deterministic**, but wastes CPU when idle and delays response to the polling interval; can be preferable when events are extremely frequent (interrupt storms → "interrupt coalescing"/NAPI switch NICs to polling under load) |
| **Buffering vs handshaking** | Buffering **stores data** to bridge speed differences; handshaking **exchanges control signals** to agree when to send. They combine: handshaking (flow control) stops the sender when the buffer is nearly full |
| **Hardware vs software handshaking/flow control** | Hardware uses dedicated wires (RTS/CTS) – fast, reliable; software uses in-band characters (XON/XOFF) – works over any link but can be confused by binary data |
| **Interrupt priority vs first-come** | Priorities ensure critical devices pre-empt; without priorities a slow printer could delay a power-failure warning |
| **Programmed I/O vs DMA** | Programmed I/O: CPU copies every word (with interrupts or polling); DMA: controller copies blocks, CPU interrupted once |
| **Spooling vs simple buffering** | Spooling queues whole jobs on disk for a shared slow device (printer) – multiple users; buffering is generally smaller and transient |

| Device / situation | Dominant technique(s) | Why |
| Keyboard, mouse (PS/2) | Interrupt | Rare, unpredictable events |
| Mouse/keyboard on USB | Host **polling** (1–8 ms) | USB design – host-controlled bus |
| Printer | **Buffer/spooler** + **handshaking** (BUSY/ACK or network flow control) + low-priority interrupt | Very slow device; many users |
| Network interface card | **Interrupts** (frame arrival) with **ring buffers**, DMA; polling mode under heavy load | Bursty high-speed traffic |
| Hard disk/SSD | Interrupt on completion + DMA + cache buffer | Block transfers |
| Video streaming | **Buffer** (seconds ahead) | Jitter tolerance |
| UPS power-loss signal | **High-priority interrupt** | Must pre-empt everything to save state |
| Serial sensor/modem | Handshaking (RTS/CTS or XON/XOFF) + buffer | Slow link flow control |
| Arduino reading a button | Polling in loop (or `attachInterrupt` for immediate response) | Simplicity vs responsiveness |

### 31.3 Determining the technique in network scenarios

| Scenario | Technique(s) | Explanation |
| Twenty learners send documents to the lab's network printer at once; PCs stay usable | **Spooling/buffering** on the print server; **handshaking** (printer signals busy/ready; TCP flow control) | Jobs queued on disk; printer pulls data at its pace |
| A file server's NIC receives thousands of frames per second | **Interrupts** with **ring buffers** and **DMA**; **interrupt coalescing/polling** under load | Efficient handling of bursts without an interrupt per frame |
| Two laptops far apart on the same Wi-Fi keep colliding until the AP enables RTS/CTS | **Handshaking** (RTS/CTS) | Channel reserved before data |
| A browser connects to a website: SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK, then data flows with window sizes advertised | **Handshaking** (TCP three-way) + **buffering/flow control** (receive window) | Connection set-up and speed matching |
| A YouTube video plays smoothly despite 4G fluctuations | **Buffer** | Pre-loaded seconds absorb jitter |
| The monitoring server checks every switch's status every 60 s via SNMP | **Polling** | Regular status checks; devices do not initiate |
| An SNMP switch sends a "port down" trap immediately | **Interrupt-like event notification** (trap) | Device-initiated alert – interrupt model |
| Power fails; the UPS signals the server via USB and a clean shutdown starts, pausing backups | **High-priority interrupt** | Critical event pre-empts lower tasks |
| A USB webcam in a video call | **Host polling** with **isochronous buffers** | USB host schedules transfers |
| Gateway collects data from 50 LoRa sensors by asking each in turn | **Polling** | Master–slave |
| A serial barcode scanner tells the PC to pause because its buffer is full | **Handshaking / flow control** (XOFF or CTS low) + **buffer** | Prevents overflow |

> EXAMPLE:
  **One print job, five mechanisms.** A teacher clicks Print on a 40-page PDF. The document is rendered and written to the **spool buffer** on the print server (the teacher continues working). The server opens a TCP connection to the printer – a **handshake** (SYN/SYN-ACK/ACK) – and sends data while the printer advertises how much its **buffer** can accept (flow control). When paper runs out, the printer sends a status message; on the server this arrives as a NIC **interrupt** whose priority is below the disk and clock interrupts but is still serviced within microseconds; the spooler pauses the job. Meanwhile the server's monitoring agent **polls** the printer every 30 s for toner level.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 31.1 – Speed-gap timeline**: CPU nanoseconds vs printer seconds, with a buffer drawn as a reservoir between a fast tap and a slow drain. **Figure 31.2 – Interrupt cycle**: running program → interrupt signal → save state → ISR → restore → resume, with a priority stack showing a network interrupt pre-empted by a power-failure interrupt. **Figure 31.3 – Polling loop vs interrupt** side by side (CPU busy-wait circle vs CPU free with a doorbell). **Figure 31.4 – Handshaking sequences**: RTS/CTS timing, XON/XOFF, TCP three-way handshake arrows.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "Buffers make transfers faster." They do not speed up the slow device – they free the fast one and smooth bursts. "Polling is always inefficient." For very high event rates or trivially simple systems, polling can be more efficient and predictable than storms of interrupts.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Mechanism detectives (45 min).** (1) Role-play: one "CPU" and five "devices"; run a polling round then an interrupt round with priority cards – count wasted CPU turns and measure response time for a "power failure" event. (2) Lab observation: print a large document and watch the spooler queue (Windows print queue / CUPS); open Task Manager/`cat /proc/interrupts` to see interrupt counts rising while moving the mouse and during a download; capture a TCP handshake in Wireshark; view the streaming buffer in a video player's stats. (3) Scenario worksheet: determine the technique(s) for twelve network/device scenarios and justify. (4) Arduino (if available): compare a button read by polling in `loop()` vs `attachInterrupt()`.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Explain the meaning and role of buffers, interrupts, interrupt priorities, polling and handshaking, each with an example.
  2. Describe what the CPU does when an interrupt arrives; why are priorities necessary?
  3. Differentiate interrupt-driven I/O from polling on CPU efficiency, latency, complexity and suitability; give a case where polling is better.
  4. Distinguish buffering from handshaking and explain how they cooperate in flow control.
  5. Determine the technique used: a network printer queue; a NIC under heavy traffic; a UPS power-loss signal; SNMP monitoring; TCP connection set-up; a video stream.
  6. What is spooling and why is it used for shared printers?
  **Homework:** Trace the sending of a photo from a phone to a friend via Wi-Fi and the internet, identifying at least one buffer, one interrupt, one polling instance and two handshakes along the way, with a labelled diagram.

---

# Lesson 32: Network Protocols and Their Roles in Data Transmission

> OBJ:
  - Explain the meaning and role of network protocols in data transmission.
  - Discuss the functions of protocols (e.g., IP, TCP, HTTP…) in communication.
  - Evaluate how TCP/IP and other protocols facilitate reliable and efficient data transmission.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min – computer room helpful)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 6 min | Two learners "phone" each other in different languages without rules; then with a script (greeting, turn-taking, confirmation). Protocol = agreed rules. | Reflect. |
| Core 1 | 20 min | Definition and roles of protocols (syntax, semantics, timing; addressing, segmentation, encapsulation, routing, error detection/correction, flow/congestion control, connection management, security); standards (IETF RFCs, IEEE, W3C); protocol suites/stacks; ports. | Notes. |
| Core 2 | 40 min | Functions of key protocols: **IP** (addressing, packetising, routing, best-effort; IPv4/IPv6), **TCP** (connection-oriented, reliable, ordered, flow/congestion control, ports), **UDP** (connectionless, fast), **HTTP/HTTPS** (web request/response, methods, status codes, TLS), **DNS** (names → IP), **DHCP** (automatic addressing), **SMTP/POP3/IMAP** (e-mail), **FTP/SFTP** (files), **SSH**, **ARP**, **ICMP** (ping/traceroute), **Ethernet/Wi-Fi** (link), **TLS**, **SMB**, **NTP**, VoIP (SIP/RTP). Demos: `ipconfig`, `nslookup`, `ping`, `tracert`, browser dev tools (HTTP headers), Wireshark capture of DNS→TCP handshake→HTTP. | Protocol cards; observe capture. |
| Core 3 | 26 min | Evaluating TCP/IP: how reliability is achieved (sequence numbers, ACKs, retransmission, checksums, ordering), efficiency (windowing, congestion control, pipelining, header compression, UDP where speed matters, QUIC/HTTP-3), scalability and interoperability (layering, open standards), weaknesses (IPv4 exhaustion, no built-in security → TLS/IPsec, latency of handshakes, head-of-line blocking) and how other protocols complement; case: a video call vs file download. | Evaluation table; debate. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 32.1 Meaning and role of network protocols

> DEF:
  A **network protocol** is a **set of agreed rules and formats** governing how devices **exchange data** – what messages look like (**syntax**), what they mean (**semantics**) and when/in what order they are sent (**timing**). Protocols allow devices from different manufacturers to interoperate; they are defined in open standards (IETF **RFCs** for internet protocols, IEEE 802 for Ethernet/Wi-Fi, W3C for web formats).

| Role of protocols | What it ensures | Example |
| **Addressing & identification** | Every device/application is reachable | IP addresses, MAC addresses, port numbers, domain names |
| **Encapsulation & framing** | Data wrapped with headers/trailers at each layer | Ethernet frame → IP packet → TCP segment → HTTP message |
| **Segmentation & reassembly** | Large messages split into manageable units and rebuilt in order | TCP segments, IP fragmentation |
| **Routing & forwarding** | Path selection across networks | IP + routing protocols (OSPF, BGP) |
| **Connection management** | Set-up, maintenance, tear-down of sessions | TCP handshake/FIN, SIP calls |
| **Error detection & recovery** | Corrupted/lost data detected and retransmitted | Checksums, CRC, ACK/retransmit |
| **Flow & congestion control** | Sender does not overwhelm receiver or network | TCP windows, slow start |
| **Sequencing** | Correct order | Sequence numbers |
| **Security** | Confidentiality, integrity, authentication | TLS, IPsec, WPA3, SSH |
| **Service functions** | Name resolution, configuration, time, e-mail transfer, file transfer | DNS, DHCP, NTP, SMTP, FTP |
| **Interoperability** | Heterogeneous systems communicate | Open standards |

### 32.2 Functions of key protocols

| Protocol | Layer (TCP/IP) | Function | Key facts |
| **IP – Internet Protocol** | Internet | **Addresses** hosts and **routes packets** between networks; best-effort, connectionless (no guarantee of delivery/order) | **IPv4** 32-bit (192.168.1.10; ~4.3 billion, exhausted → NAT), **IPv6** 128-bit (2001:db8::1); header has source/destination, TTL; routers forward hop by hop |
| **TCP – Transmission Control Protocol** | Transport | **Reliable, ordered, connection-oriented** delivery between applications (ports): three-way handshake, sequence numbers, acknowledgements, retransmission, checksum, flow control (window), congestion control | Used by web, e-mail, file transfer, SSH; port 80/443/25/22 |
| **UDP – User Datagram Protocol** | Transport | **Connectionless, fast, no reliability** – minimal header; application handles losses | DNS, VoIP/video calls (RTP), streaming, gaming, DHCP; port 53/67 |
| **HTTP / HTTPS – HyperText Transfer Protocol (Secure)** | Application | **Client–server request/response** for web resources: methods GET/POST/PUT/DELETE, headers, status codes (200 OK, 301, 404, 500); HTTPS adds **TLS** encryption/authentication | Port 80 / **443**; HTTP/2 multiplexing, HTTP/3 over QUIC (UDP) |
| **DNS – Domain Name System** | Application | Translates **names to IP addresses** (www.minesec.gov.cm → 41.x.x.x) via hierarchical servers (root → .cm → minesec.gov.cm); caching | UDP/TCP 53; record types A, AAAA, MX, CNAME |
| **DHCP – Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol** | Application | Automatically assigns **IP address, mask, gateway, DNS** to devices (Discover–Offer–Request–Ack) | UDP 67/68; leases |
| **SMTP / POP3 / IMAP** | Application | E-mail: **SMTP** sends/relays (port 25/587), **POP3** downloads (110/995), **IMAP** synchronises folders on the server (143/993) | Secured with TLS |
| **FTP / SFTP / FTPS** | Application | File transfer; FTP plaintext (20/21) – replaced by **SFTP** (over SSH, 22) or FTPS | Uploading websites, backups |
| **SSH** | Application | Encrypted remote command line and tunnels | Port 22; replaces Telnet |
| **TLS/SSL** | Between transport and application | Encryption, integrity, server/client authentication via certificates | Underlies HTTPS, SMTPS, IMAPS, VPNs |
| **ARP** | Link/Internet boundary | Maps IP address → MAC address on a LAN | `arp -a` |
| **ICMP** | Internet | Diagnostics and errors: **ping** (echo), **traceroute**, "destination unreachable", TTL exceeded | Not for data transfer |
| **Ethernet (IEEE 802.3) / Wi-Fi (802.11)** | Network access/link | Framing, MAC addressing, media access (CSMA/CD/CA), error detection (CRC) on the local link | Switches and APs operate here |
| **SMB/CIFS, NFS** | Application | File and printer sharing on LANs | Windows shares, Samba |
| **NTP** | Application | Clock synchronisation | Logs, certificates depend on time |
| **SIP / RTP** | Application | VoIP call set-up (SIP) and real-time media transport (RTP over UDP) | WhatsApp/Zoom-like services use similar |
| **SNMP** | Application | Network device monitoring/management | Polling and traps (Lesson 31) |
| Routing protocols (OSPF, BGP) | Internet | Routers exchange routes; BGP glues the internet's networks | ISPs |

**Encapsulation example (loading a web page):** browser HTTP GET → TCP segment (src port 52344, dst 443) → IP packet (src 192.168.1.10, dst 172.217.x.x) → Ethernet frame (src MAC PC, dst MAC router) → bits on UTP; the router strips the frame, routes the packet; the server reverses the process. Before this: DHCP gave the address, DNS resolved the name, ARP found the router's MAC, TCP/TLS handshakes established the session.

### 32.3 Evaluating how TCP/IP and other protocols facilitate reliable and efficient transmission

| Goal | How TCP/IP achieves it | Complementary protocols | Limitations & mitigations |
| **Reliability** | TCP: **sequence numbers** order segments; **acknowledgements** confirm receipt; **retransmission** after time-out or duplicate ACKs; **checksums** detect corruption; connection state ensures completeness | Link-layer **CRC** discards corrupted frames early; Wi-Fi link ACKs; TLS integrity | IP alone is unreliable by design (keeps routers simple); UDP applications must add their own recovery (or accept loss – voice) |
| **Efficiency** | **Sliding window** sends many segments before waiting for ACKs; **congestion control** (slow start, congestion avoidance) adapts to network capacity; **Nagle/delayed ACK** reduce small packets; large MTU/jumbo frames; **UDP** for low-overhead real-time traffic; **HTTP/2 multiplexing**, **HTTP/3/QUIC** cut handshake latency | DNS caching; compression (HTTP gzip); CDNs; header compression | TCP handshake + TLS add round trips (bad on 600 ms satellite); **head-of-line blocking**; bufferbloat; mitigations: QUIC, TCP Fast Open, tuned windows, edge caching |
| **Scalability & routing** | IP's hierarchical addressing and routers forward billions of packets; BGP/OSPF find paths; TTL prevents loops | NAT stretched IPv4; **IPv6** solves exhaustion | IPv4 exhaustion, NAT breaks end-to-end; IPv6 adoption uneven in Cameroon |
| **Interoperability & flexibility** | **Layering**: any application over TCP/UDP, IP over any link (Ethernet, Wi-Fi, 4G, fibre, satellite); open RFC standards | Ethernet/802.11 standards; DNS/DHCP automate configuration | Layer violations by middleboxes (NAT, firewalls) complicate new protocols |
| **Security** | Not built into original TCP/IP | **TLS/HTTPS, IPsec/VPN, SSH, WPA3, DNSSEC** add confidentiality/integrity/authentication | Plain HTTP/FTP/Telnet still exist; spoofing of IP/ARP; DDoS |
| **Quality of service** | Best-effort IP; DiffServ marking | Wi-Fi WMM, switch QoS, RTP timing | Congestion still hurts real-time traffic |
| **Verdict** | TCP/IP delivers reliable transport on top of unreliable networks efficiently enough to run the global internet; the trade-off is complexity in end hosts and latency from handshakes | – | Modern evolution (QUIC, IPv6, TLS 1.3) addresses the main weaknesses |

Case: **file download vs video call.** The 2 GB download uses **TCP** – every byte must arrive in order; delays are acceptable; congestion control shares the line fairly. The video call uses **UDP/RTP** – a lost packet is skipped rather than retransmitted (a late frame is useless); jitter buffers and forward error correction maintain quality; SIP over TCP/UDP sets up the call. Choosing the right transport protocol per application is itself an efficiency decision.

> EXAMPLE:
  **Registering for the GCE online from a cybercafé in Maroua.** DHCP gives the PC an address; DNS resolves the Board's portal; ARP finds the router; TCP's three-way handshake then TLS handshake set up a secure session (≈ 4 round trips – noticeable on the café's satellite backup link); HTTPS GET/POST carry the form; TCP retransmits segments lost on a noisy 4G hop so the payment confirmation arrives intact; ICMP lets the technician `ping`/`tracert` when the link drops. Protocol design – reliability where it matters, tolerance elsewhere – is why the registration succeeds over an imperfect network.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 32.1 – Encapsulation stack**: HTTP message inside TCP segment inside IP packet inside Ethernet frame, with header fields (ports, IPs, MACs) shown and the devices that read each (switch – MAC, router – IP, server – port). **Figure 32.2 – TCP reliability diagram**: three-way handshake, segments with sequence numbers, ACKs, a lost segment retransmitted, sliding window. **Figure 32.3 – Protocol map by function**: addressing (IP, ARP, DNS, DHCP), transport (TCP, UDP), applications (HTTP, SMTP/IMAP, FTP/SFTP, SSH, SMB, SIP/RTP), security (TLS, IPsec, WPA3), diagnostics (ICMP, SNMP). **Figure 32.4 – Wireshark capture** annotated: DNS query/response, SYN/SYN-ACK/ACK, TLS Client Hello, HTTP/2 data.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "IP guarantees delivery." IP is best-effort; TCP provides reliability. "HTTPS is a different protocol from HTTP." It is HTTP carried inside TLS. "UDP is bad because it loses data." For real-time media, timeliness beats completeness – UDP is the right choice.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Protocols in action (50 min, pairs, lab).** (1) Commands: `ipconfig /all` (DHCP lease, DNS), `nslookup www.camtel.cm`, `arp -a`, `ping`, `tracert 8.8.8.8` – annotate which protocol each uses. (2) Browser dev tools ▸ Network: open a page; record request method, status codes, headers, TLS version, number of connections. (3) Wireshark (lab filter `dns || tcp.flags.syn==1 || tls.handshake`): capture a page load; identify DNS, three-way handshake, TLS handshake; count retransmissions during a deliberately degraded link. (4) Role-play "reliable delivery": send a 10-card message across the room with a lossy courier; implement sequence numbers, ACKs and retransmission; then a "UDP" round for a live "video" – discuss. (5) Evaluation paragraph: how TCP/IP made your capture reliable and where efficiency was lost (handshakes, retransmissions).

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Explain what a network protocol is and describe six roles protocols play in data transmission.
  2. Discuss the functions of IP, TCP, UDP, HTTP/HTTPS, DNS and DHCP.
  3. Describe the encapsulation of an HTTP request down to the Ethernet frame and name the device that reads each address.
  4. Explain how TCP achieves reliable transmission (four mechanisms) and how it achieves efficiency (two mechanisms).
  5. Evaluate TCP/IP: two strengths, two weaknesses and the protocols that mitigate the weaknesses.
  6. Why do video calls use UDP while downloads use TCP?
  **Homework:** Produce a one-page "protocol journey" comic/diagram of sending an e-mail with an attachment from Gmail to a friend on Yahoo, naming every protocol involved in order (DHCP, DNS, TCP, TLS, HTTPS, SMTP, IMAP…) and where reliability and security are provided.

